# Research Brief Multi-Agent Pipeline (Macroscope test repo)

A small multi-agent system for testing AI code-review tools. A `Planner`
agent splits a free-text brief into subtasks, a `Researcher` agent fetches
and scores source material for each subtask concurrently, a `Writer` agent
drafts a paragraph per subtask, and a `Critic` agent checks each draft
against configurable rules, looping back to the writer when a draft fails.

```
Planner -> Researcher (parallel) -> Writer -> Critic -> (loop) -> done
```

## Running it

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python main.py "vector databases, agent memory, tool routing"
pytest tests/
```

Both commands succeed, and the test suite passes. That's intentional.

## Why this repo exists

This codebase has several realistic bugs seeded into it on purpose,
spanning data integrity, security, concurrency, and correctness issues —
similar in flavor to issues that have come up in real review (e.g., the
binary-classifier label-handling bug in `tools/scoring.py` mirrors a
`probability(yes)`/`probability(no)`-style bug from a PMML scorer).

Push this to a repo with Macroscope installed, open a PR, and see what it
flags versus what the unit tests miss. `SEEDED_BUGS.md` has the answer key
— don't open it until after you've seen what the review tool catches, or
you'll bias your own read on precision/recall.
